A `for` loop calls `resolveLinkedEntity()` (a DB `SELECT`) then `db.update(timeBlocks)` **per accepted block**. A 20-block schedule acceptance triggers up to 40 DB round-trips.

```
apps/lambdas/planner-lambda/src/routes/acceptSchedule.ts:182
```

```typescript
// CURRENT — N+1
for (const block of created) {
  const link = await resolveLinkedEntity({ id: block.id, ... }) // SELECT per block
  if (!link) continue
  await db.update(timeBlocks).set({ linkedEntityId: link.id, ... }) // UPDATE per block
}

// FIX — batch lookup then single bulkUpdate
const resolvable = created.filter(b => ['WORKOUT','MEAL','SUPPLEMENT'].includes(b.type))
const links = await Promise.all(resolvable.map(b => resolveLinkedEntity(...).catch(() => null)))
const toUpdate = resolvable.flatMap((b, i) => links[i] ? [{ id: b.id, link: links[i]! }] : [])
// Then batch: Promise.all(toUpdate.map(({ id, link }) => db.update(...).where(eq(timeBlocks.id, id))))
```

---

For every block in `allBlocks` (potentially 50+), `pauseRows.find()` scans all supplement pauses. This is O(n×m) on every request. Fix with a pre-computed `Map`:

```
apps/lambdas/planner-lambda/src/routes/getWeek.ts:276
```

```typescript
// CURRENT — O(n × |pauseRows|) scan per block
for (const b of allBlocks) {
  const overlap = pauseRows.find(
    (p) => p.userSupplementId === supplementId && p.pausedFrom <= scheduledDate && p.resumesOn >= scheduledDate,
  )
}

// FIX — precompute Map<supplementId, PauseRow[]> once before the loop
const pauseBySupplementId = new Map<string, typeof pauseRows>()
for (const p of pauseRows) {
  if (!pauseBySupplementId.has(p.userSupplementId)) pauseBySupplementId.set(p.userSupplementId, [])
  pauseBySupplementId.get(p.userSupplementId)!.push(p)
}

for (const b of allBlocks) {
  const candidatePauses = pauseBySupplementId.get(supplementId) ?? []
  const overlap = candidatePauses.find(p => p.pausedFrom <= scheduledDate && p.resumesOn >= scheduledDate)
}
```

After computing `target`, the stage does: `SELECT nutrition_targets → UPDATE/INSERT → UPDATE goal_settings → SELECT training_phases → INSERT training_phase → seedDefaultPantry()`. The two reads (`SELECT nutrition_targets` at line 370 and `SELECT training_phases` at line 414) can run in parallel with each other:

```typescript
// FIX
const [existingTarget, activePhase] = await Promise.all([
  db.select({ id: nutritionTargets.id }).from(nutritionTargets).where(eq(nutritionTargets.userId, userId)).limit(1),
  db.select({ id: trainingPhases.id }).from(trainingPhases).where(and(eq(trainingPhases.userId, userId), isNull(trainingPhases.endedAt))).limit(1),
])
// Then: UPDATE/INSERT targets + UPDATE goalSettings + conditionally INSERT training_phase
// seedDefaultPantry can also run concurrently with goal_settings update
```

---

The inner `existing.find()` and `accepted.find()` on every iteration make the conflict check O(n²). Acceptable for current schedule sizes (~30 blocks) but worth noting:

```typescript
// FIX for production scale — build an interval tree or precompute sorted array
// For now: at least replace accepted.find with a timeline scan using the existing sorted insert
// (The existing.find scan against DB rows is the bigger issue — that's already one bulk SELECT at line 97)
```

